# Windows Terminal writes settings.json with LF and rewrites the whole file on
# every save. With core.autocrlf=true the working tree copy becomes CRLF, so
# setup_windows.ps1 sees a difference on every run, copies, and -Pull hands the
# file back as LF -- an endless flip that buries real changes in noise.
.config/windows-terminal/LocalState/settings.json text eol=lf

# setup.sh only ever runs on the Linux box, but core.autocrlf=true gives the
# Windows checkout a CRLF copy. Editing it from there and committing is fine --
# git normalises on the way in -- but a heredoc in a CRLF script breaks the
# moment anyone runs it through Git Bash or WSL, and it fails inside the
# heredoc rather than at the shebang, which is a bad place to debug from.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for everything else that only ever runs on the Linux side. A
# systemd unit with CRLF fails to parse, and the failure names the directive
# rather than the line ending.
*.py text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf

# Vendored skills are gitignored (see .claude/skills/VENDOR). Nothing here.
